<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leopold I, Margrave of Austria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 994</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Leopold the Illustrious · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-babenberg">House of Babenberg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leopold I, called the Illustrious, was the first Babenberg ruler of the eastern march of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Emperor Otto II gave him the march in 976, and he pushed its frontier east along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melk">Melk</a>. He was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> in 994, and his descendants ruled Austria until 1246.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leopold-i-margrave-of-austria.jpg" alt="Leopold I, Margrave of Austria" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Part · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herzog_Leopold_I._Babenberg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leopold was born about 940, and his origins are not known. According to some sources his father Berthold was count in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nordgau">Nordgau</a>, north of Regensburg; a more recent theory makes him a younger son of Duke Arnulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. His ancestry remains disputed, though descent from the Popponids, the Elder House of Babenberg, is assumed. He is first mentioned in a document of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> of 13 February 962 as Liupo, count of the Bavarian Donaugau and of the Traungau, and a faithful follower of the Emperor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After his victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld">Battle of Lechfeld</a> in 955, Otto I re-established the Eastern March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> under Margrave Burkhard. Burkhard joined the uprising of Duke Henry II against Otto II and was deposed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a> of Regensburg in 976. By a charter of 21 July 976 Leopold was appointed margrave of the Eastern March, the core of the later Archduchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. The resettlement of the east was slow and centred on the fortress of Pöchlarn down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. His margraviate at first coincided with the Wachau valley, bounded in the east by the Traisen.</p><p>With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> threat much reduced, Leopold concentrated on securing his holdings against internal threats. In 984 he reduced the fortress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melk">Melk</a>, still held by supporters of the late margrave, and probably made it his residence, founding a monastery there for twelve secular priests. By 987 his boundary reached the Wienerwald, and by 991, jointly with the reinstated Duke Henry II, it reached the Fischa river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 994 Leopold went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> to mediate between his cousin Margrave Henry of Schweinfurt and Bishop Bernward, one of whose knights Henry had seized and blinded. At a tournament on 8 July he was hit in the eye by an arrow aimed at his cousin, and he died on 10 July 994. He was buried in Würzburg; his son Ernest I, Duke of Swabia, was buried beside him in 1015, and in the 13th century their remains were returned to Melk Abbey. The chronicler Thietmar wrote that no man was wiser in all his actions. Otto III at once gave his son Henry I the margraviate.</p><p>Leopold married Richardis, and they had eight children, among them Henry I, Adalbert, the third margrave, and Poppo, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>. In 1976 the thousandth anniversary of his appointment was marked as a Thousand Years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leopold I, Margrave of Austria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 976 Otto II gave it to <b>Leopold of Babenberg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-austria">Margraviate of Austria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.79,9.93&amp;year=984" aria-label="Würzburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4778 430 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Leopold I, Margrave of Austria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5228 700h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5228 700h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.79,9.93&amp;year=984">What was here in 984 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
